{"name":"Netsuke","hex":"#e0cfb0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"netsuke","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, often collectible, artistic carving characterized by an opening or two small holes (紐通し (himotōshi)), most commonly made of wood or ivory, used as a fob at the end of a cord attached to a suspended pouch containing pens, medicines, or tobacco. Netsuke originated in feudal Japan in the late 16th and 17th centuries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/netsuke"}],"entries":[{"title":"Netsuke","blocks":[{"text":"A netsuke  is a miniature sculpture, originating in 17th century Japan. Initially a simply-carved button fastener on the cords of an inrō box, netsuke later developed into ornately sculpted objects of craftsmanship."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Ivory_netsuke_of_the_Hare_with_Amber_Eyes_2_%28cropped%29.JPG/330px-Ivory_netsuke_of_the_Hare_with_Amber_Eyes_2_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netsuke","wikidata":"Q543901"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/%E5%8D%B0%E7%B1%A0-Inro_with_Design_of_Eulalia_Grass_and_Deer.jpg/500px-%E5%8D%B0%E7%B1%A0-Inro_with_Design_of_Eulalia_Grass_and_Deer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/%E5%8D%B0%E7%B1%A0-Inro_with_Design_of_Eulalia_Grass_and_Deer.jpg/1280px-%E5%8D%B0%E7%B1%A0-Inro_with_Design_of_Eulalia_Grass_and_Deer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/%E5%8D%B0%E7%B1%A0-Inro_with_Design_of_Eulalia_Grass_and_Deer.jpg/500px-%E5%8D%B0%E7%B1%A0-Inro_with_Design_of_Eulalia_Grass_and_Deer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An ivory netsuke with an e